// SPDX-License-Identifier: zlib
// See ../FORK-LICENSE.md for what that identifier covers.
//
//! The build half of zlib's runtime-loading route: three modules and one
//! generated file.
//!
//! An SDK that ships the runtime route calls `createModule` in place of
//! `translate.createModule()` and imports what comes back as its `c` module.
//! Nothing here links the native library. That is the point: in this
//! configuration the symbols arrive through the platform loader at run time,
//! and linking the archive as well would make the linker demand them before
//! `load` ever runs.
//!
//!   rt        runtime/loader.zig, the reusable core. Pure Zig; imported by the
//!             facade in both configurations, because `LoadSource` and
//!             `LoadError` are this SDK's public types and must be the same
//!             types whichever configuration is built.
//!   library   runtime/library.zig, this SDK's descriptor: the namespaced
//!             synchronization binding and the four load-time checks.
//!   dispatch  generated by runtime/generate.zig from the real translation
//!             output: the table, the wrappers, `load`, and the re-exported
//!             translated namespace. It imports `rt`, `library` and the
//!             generator's patched translation as `c_native`, which imports the
//!             dispatch back as `c_routed`.
//!   sync.c    compiled into the dispatch with `-DRT_SYNC_PREFIX=zlib_rt`, so
//!             its symbols cannot collide with another SDK's.
//!
//! ## The one thing a caller must not do
//!
//! Do not link the compiled C archive into a module that imports this dispatch.
//! The facade's `c` import is the dispatch, and nothing else in the graph should
//! reference the library, or the linker resolves the symbols statically and the
//! runtime route silently becomes a linked one.
//!
//! ## What is routed, and what is not
//!
//! The translated header for this SDK is `zig/translate.h`, which includes
//! `<zlib.h>` and `<zconf.h>` and defines no macro. `zlib.h` itself includes
//! `zconf.h` (`src/zlib.h:33-38`), so naming both reaches the same declarations
//! twice over, with the second include a no-op behind the header's own guard.
//!
//! Every `extern` the translation produces is routed except the ones named in
//! `libc_noise` below: those stay direct. `provider_optional` and
//! `provider_excluded` are empty, and are written out rather than omitted so
//! that adding a header with an optional or deliberately-direct entry point is
//! a change in this file with a name in it. A provider that does not export
//! zlib's whole declared API is refused rather than half used.
//!
//! ## Where `libc_noise` comes from, and what it is worth
//!
//! **Method: text analysis of the pinned Zig 0.16.0 distribution's system
//! headers, not a translation.** `zig translate-c` emits a declaration for
//! every function it parses in the include closure, so the C library's own
//! surface reaches the translation alongside zlib's. The closure here was
//! collected by starting from the five system headers that zlib's two public
//! headers can reach in this configuration and following the `#include` graph
//! textually, across the libc families this SDK can be built for:
//! `generic-glibc`, `generic-musl`, `wasm-wasi-musl`, `any-windows-any`,
//! `any-darwin-any`, `generic-freebsd`, `generic-netbsd` and `generic-openbsd`,
//! all under `lib/libc/include` of the compiler named in `UPSTREAM.md`. Every
//! identifier that looks like a function declaration was collected, then every
//! identifier defined by a `#define` in the same closure was subtracted.
//!
//! The five roots, each read out of `src/zconf.h` rather than guessed:
//!
//!   `<stddef.h>`    `src/zconf.h:264`, the `z_size_t` definition, on the
//!                   `STDC` path; and `src/zconf.h:468` on `_WIN32`.
//!   `<limits.h>`    `src/zconf.h:430`, the `Z_U4` selection.
//!   `<sys/types.h>` `src/zconf.h:456`, "for off_t".
//!   `<stdarg.h>`    `src/zconf.h:462`, "for va_list", reached by `zprintf`
//!                   and `gzprintf`.
//!   `<unistd.h>`    `src/zconf.h:490`, "for SEEK_*, off_t", reachable only
//!                   because the *configured* `zconf.h` this build generates has
//!                   `HAVE_UNISTD_H` switched on at `:446`. That is the whole
//!                   reason the generated header has to be on the translation's
//!                   include line and not just the library's.
//!
//! Note what is *not* in the closure. zlib's public headers reach no
//! `<stdio.h>`, no `<stdlib.h>` and no `<string.h>`: `src/zconf.h` and
//! `src/zlib.h` together include nothing else, and `src/gzguts.h` - which does
//! include all three at `:32,35,36` - is an internal header that no unit
//! `zig/translate.h` names. This SDK's closure is therefore much smaller than a
//! Lua-shaped one's would be.
//!
//! **What this list is: a reasoned superset, not an observed translation.** It
//! was not produced by running `zig translate-c`, so it is neither necessary
//! nor sufficient for any particular target. Two properties make it usable
//! anyway:
//!
//!   * **Over-listing is safe.** A name a given target's headers do not
//!     declare simply never appears in that target's translation, so the entry
//!     costs nothing and matches nothing.
//!   * **Under-listing is not.** A libc name that *does* appear in the
//!     translation and is missing from this list gets routed, and the provider
//!     is then asked for a symbol it does not export - a load-time
//!     `MissingSymbol` on every load.
//!
//! The union is taken across all eight families precisely because the
//! asymmetry is that forgiving: a name present for only one target costs
//! nothing on the other seven, while omitting a name that one of them needs
//! costs everything on it.
//!
//! **The first gate that can observe a shortfall is
//! `zig build test -Druntime=true` with a provider named.** Nothing shorter
//! reaches `dlsym`: a linked build never consults this list at all, so a
//! missing libc name is invisible until a runtime-loaded build is actually run
//! against a real `libz.so`. That gate is `PENDING-VERIFICATION`; this package
//! has not run it. It is recorded as an open risk in `FORK-NOTES.md`.
//!
//! ### The list is comma-separated, and every line ends with one
//!
//! `runtime/generate.zig` reads these three lists through
//! `std.mem.splitScalar(u8, list, ',')` followed by a whitespace trim, and a
//! Zig multiline string literal concatenates its lines with nothing between
//! them. A line that does not end in a comma therefore glues its last name to
//! the first name of the next line into one token, and that token matches
//! neither declaration: both real names are lost and both get routed. The
//! commas are load-bearing, not decoration.

const std = @import("std");

/// The prefix `runtime/sync.c` is compiled with.
///
/// `runtime/library.zig` declares the same prefix in the symbol names it binds.
/// The two must agree, and a build where they do not fails to link, which is
/// the whole error report: there is no runtime path that could hide it.
///
/// A build that needs a second, independent instance of this SDK in one binary
/// should change this value *and* the prefix in `runtime/library.zig`, and then
/// give the second instance its own copy of both files. Nothing else in the
/// package assumes one instance.
const sync_prefix = "zlib_rt";

/// `extern` declarations that are the C library's, not zlib's.
///
/// See the module comment for where this list comes from, why leaving a name
/// out of it is a load-time failure rather than a detail, and which gate would
/// observe a shortfall. Every line below ends in a comma; see the note above.
